# AGENTS.md

## General guidelines

- Do not add `Co-Authored-By` to git commits.
- Whenever the user asks you to commit changes, use the global `gitmoji` skill.
- Use any available package manager, such as Pixi, uv, or npm, when dependencies or tools are needed. Keep all installations and related files—including dependencies, virtual environments, binaries, runtimes, and caches—inside the current working directory. Never install them globally, system-wide, or elsewhere in the user's home directory.

## Communication style

Apply this to every reply, in any language.

- Lead with the answer or result. Keep replies as short as the question allows, with only what the user needs to act or decide.
- Write English prose in clear, natural language at about CEFR B2 level for someone who uses English as a second language. Use familiar words without sounding childish or adding deliberate mistakes. Follow a supplied writing sample or a different level when the user requests one.

## Persian writing style

Apply these preferences across all projects, but only to Persian-language passages. Do not apply them to English or other non-Persian text, including when multiple languages appear in the same response. In English and other non-Persian text, use ASCII digits (`0-9`) and Gregorian dates unless the user explicitly requests another format.

- Use clear, natural, consistent Persian and match the requested formal or conversational tone.
- Do not use optional vowel marks, tanwin, tashdid, or decorative kashida. Write the ezafe after final heh as «ه‌ی», such as «صفحه‌ی» and «مجموعه‌ی», instead of «هٔ» or «ۀ».
- Prefer established spellings without hamza or Arabic-style forms, such as «تایید»، «تاثیر»، «سوال»، «مسوول» and «ویدیو». Preserve meaning and proper names; do not blindly delete letters or replace Arabic-origin vocabulary. Keep Persian letters such as «آ» intact.
- Use Persian «ی» and «ک» instead of Arabic «ي» and «ك». Use correct half-spaces, such as «می‌شود»، «نمی‌توان»، «مقاله‌ها» and «به‌روزرسانی».
- Use Persian punctuation («،»، «؛»، «؟») and quotation marks («…»), with consistent spacing and no repeated spaces.
- Use Persian digits («۰۱۲۳۴۵۶۷۸۹») in human-readable Persian text.
- Keep Persian lines readable in right-to-left display: do not label items with Latin letters such as a, b, c (for figure panels, write «پنل اول»، «پنل دوم» and so on, with the letter in parentheses if needed, such as «پنل اول (a)»), and do not start a line, heading or list item with a Latin word or code. Prefer the Persian word over an English abbreviation when a familiar one exists.
- Display human-readable Persian dates in the Solar Hijri (Jalali) calendar with Persian digits. Convert dates accurately, preserve the intended date and timezone, and clarify missing date components when they cannot be inferred reliably.
- Preserve technical formats: do not alter code, identifiers, URLs or slugs, file paths, commands, API values, machine-readable numbers or dates (including ISO 8601 dates in metadata), or exact quotations and names when fidelity matters. Apply these preferences to requested content changes without rewriting unrelated existing text.
